<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel">
<li id="S2.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Statistics for V1 and V2 include both PyPI and NPM datasets.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Version normalization (V2) uses the latest version for all packages.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Relationships originate from their respective ecosystems and point to nodes in arbitrary ecosystems (e.g., PyPI → nodes, NPM → nodes).</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The construction process, summarized in <a href="#alg1" title="1 ‣ II-B LLM Supply Chain Construction ‣ II Dataset and Supply Chain Construction ‣ Understanding Large Language Model Supply Chain: Structure, Domain, and Vulnerabilities" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm 1</span></a>, begins with parsing package names and their associated version information from a local dataset <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each unique package-version pair is represented as a distinct node in the graph. Once the nodes are initialized, we resolve dependencies for each package-version node. For dependencies available locally, edges are directly established to link the package to its dependencies. For dependencies not found in the local dataset, external APIs <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> are queried to fetch the missing metadata. If the queried dependency is classified as an LLM-relevant package, a new node will be added to the graph along with the corresponding edge linking it to the dependent package. In cases where dependency declarations lack specific version information or dependency data are unavailable locally, the latest version of the package is used as a fallback for resolution, mitigating issues caused by incomplete or ambiguous dependency declarations.
Finally, we incorporate vulnerability information collected in <a href="#S2.SS1" title="II-A Dataset Collection and Pre-process ‣ II Dataset and Supply Chain Construction ‣ Understanding Large Language Model Supply Chain: Structure, Domain, and Vulnerabilities" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§ II-A</span></a>, creating corresponding vulnerability nodes and affecting relationships. The resulting graph comprises 61,965 nodes and 26,736 edges, including 61,785 package-version nodes (56,788 from PyPI and 4,997 from NPM) and 180 unique vulnerabilities, as summarized in <a href="#S2.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ II-B LLM Supply Chain Construction ‣ II Dataset and Supply Chain Construction ‣ Understanding Large Language Model Supply Chain: Structure, Domain, and Vulnerabilities" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table II</span></a>. This graph provides a comprehensive representation of the LLM software ecosystem, enabling further analysis of its dependency structures and software supply chain risks.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RQ1: Structure</span></h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Methodology</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate the structure of the LLMSC, we focus on three key aspects: the composition of dependency trees, the topological structures within these trees, and the interconnections between different trees. First, we identify all distinct dependency trees in the LLMSC, treating each as a rooted directed acyclic graph (DAG), where the root node represents the entry package and edges indicate dependency relationships. For each tree, we calculate its depth (longest path from root to leaf) and size (total number of nodes) to analyze their distribution and characterize the overall structure of the LLMSC, reporting properties such as mean, median, and outliers. Second, we examine the topological structures within these trees, classifying subtrees into four main categories: chain (linear sequences), star (central nodes with multiple direct dependents), mesh (densely connected subgraphs), and diamond (nodes with shared intermediate dependencies). Using graph traversal algorithms, we calculate the proportions of these structures and evaluate their impact on dependency resolution and the stability of the ecosystem. Finally, we analyze how different dependency trees interconnect by identifying shared nodes and dependency paths, which serve as bridges between otherwise independent trees. For shared nodes, we calculate their frequency and the average number of trees they belong to, while for shared paths, we measure the fraction of reused paths across the LLMSC.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Results</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1.1:</span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">What are the overall structural characteristics of the LLMSC?</span>
To comprehensively analyze the structural characteristics of the LLMSC, we focus on the dependency trees within the graph. Each dependency tree represents a potential dependency chain, modeled as a rooted DAG where root nodes serve as entry points and edges indicate dependency relationships. Through multiple analytical perspectives, we provide a comprehensive examination of the overall structural characteristics of the LLMSC.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dependency Tree Size and Distribution.</span>
As listed in <a href="#S3.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ III-B Results ‣ III RQ1: Structure ‣ Understanding Large Language Model Supply Chain: Structure, Domain, and Vulnerabilities" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table III</span></a>, the LLMSC contains a total of 404 dependency trees, ranging from small trees with only a single node to large, complex trees with up to 3,094 nodes. The average size of a dependency tree is 37.34 nodes, with a median size of 2 nodes. This indicates that while a majority of the trees are small, a few large trees dominate the structure. The size distribution exhibits a heavy-tailed pattern: the majority of trees (79.7%) contain fewer than 5 nodes, while a small number of large trees contribute disproportionately to the overall node count. Notably, the ten largest trees alone account for 77.66% of all nodes. These larger trees often represent highly interconnected components with significant influence on the ecosystem’s behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dependency Tree Depth and Path Lengths.</span>
The depth of a dependency tree represents the longest path from the root to a leaf node, providing insight into the complexity of dependency chains. The deepest tree in the LLMSC has a depth of 6, while the average depth is 2.37. Most trees exhibit shallow depths, with 92.33% of trees having a depth of 3 or less. However, the existence of deep trees suggests that some packages rely on long dependency chains, which could increase the risk of dependency resolution issues or cascading failures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Node Degree Distribution and Critical Hubs.</span>
The degree distribution of nodes within the dependency trees reveals important structural patterns. Most nodes in the LLMSC have low degree centrality, with 54.91% of nodes acting as leaf nodes (degree = 1). However, a small number of nodes function as hubs, with degrees as high as 1,891. These hub nodes are critical connectors within the trees, linking multiple subtrees and serving as key points of dependency resolution. Their centrality makes them highly influential, but also potential single points of failure if compromised.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Isolated Trees.</span>
The LLMSC also contains a significant number of isolated dependency trees, accounting for 25.74% of all trees. These isolated trees typically consist of standalone packages or small groups of related packages that do not depend on or are not depended upon by other parts of the ecosystem. While these trees are not central to the overall structure, they represent independent functional units and reduce the complexity of dependency management for those specific packages.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TABLE III</span>: </span><span id="S3.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Statistics of dependency trees in the LLMSC.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S3.T3.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T3.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T3.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metric</span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T3.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Value</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Total number of dependency trees</td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">404</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.5.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.5.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Largest tree size (number of nodes)</td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">3,094</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.5.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.5.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Smallest tree size (number of nodes)</td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">2</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.5.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.5.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Average tree size (number of nodes)</td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">37</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.5.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.5.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Median tree size (number of nodes)</td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">2</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.5.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.5.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Longest tree depth</td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">6</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.5.8" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.5.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Average tree depth</td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">2.37</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.5.9" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.5.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Maximum node degree (hub node degree)</td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">1,891</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.5.10" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.5.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Percentage of leaf nodes (degree = 1)</td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">54.91%</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.5.11" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.5.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Percentage of isolated trees</td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">25.74%</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1.2:</span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">What are the topological substructures within the dependency trees of LLMSC, and how prevalent are they?</span>
To investigate the internal composition of dependency trees within the LLMSC, we analyze the topological substructures that shape the ecosystem’s functionality. These substructures provide insight into how dependencies are organized and distributed, revealing patterns of modularity, centralization, and connectivity.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2504.20763v1/topology.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/113;" width="405" height="113" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Representative examples of topological substructures in dependency trees. An edge pointing from A to B indicates that B depends on A, or equivalently, B is a dependent of A.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Formal Definitions of Substructures.</span>
To better understand the organization and interaction of packages within the LLMSC dependency trees, we provide formal definitions of several key topological substructures. These substructures capture distinct patterns of connectivity and modularity, offering insights into how dependencies are distributed and reused across the ecosystem. As shown in <a href="#S3.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ III-B Results ‣ III RQ1: Structure ‣ Understanding Large Language Model Supply Chain: Structure, Domain, and Vulnerabilities" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 2</span></a>, these substructures are formally defined as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Chain Topology)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A chain is the simplest substructure, representing a strictly linear sequence of dependencies without any branching. Formally, it is defined as a simple path <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=(v_{1},v_{2},\ldots,v_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=(v_{1},v_{2},\ldots,v_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, where each node <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the following conditions:</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The first node </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and the last node </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are leaf nodes, satisfying </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="deg(v_{1})=deg(v_{n})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">deg(v_{1})=deg(v_{n})=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">All intermediate nodes </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (</span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&lt;i&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&lt;i&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">) have exactly one incoming edge and one outgoing edge, satisfying </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="deg(v_{i})=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">deg(v_{i})=2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Triangle Topology)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A triangle is a closed loop of three nodes <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1},v_{2},v_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1},v_{2},v_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, where every pair of nodes is directly connected by an edge. Formally, a triangle is defined as:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(v_{1},v_{2}),(v_{1},v_{3}),(v_{2},v_{3})\}\subseteq E." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(v_{1},v_{2}),(v_{1},v_{3}),(v_{2},v_{3})\}\subseteq E.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Triangles indicate tightly coupled dependency clusters, reflecting strong interconnections between components.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Diamond Topology)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A diamond topology consists of two paths <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}=(v_{1},v_{2},v_{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}=(v_{1},v_{2},v_{4})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}=(v_{1},v_{3},v_{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}=(v_{1},v_{3},v_{4})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}\neq v_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}\neq v_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Formally, a diamond is defined as:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(v_{1},v_{2}),(v_{2},v_{4}),(v_{1},v_{3}),(v_{3},v_{4})\}\subseteq E." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(v_{1},v_{2}),(v_{2},v_{4}),(v_{1},v_{3}),(v_{3},v_{4})\}\subseteq E.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Diamonds are prevalent in shared libraries, where intermediate dependencies are reused across multiple packages.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Star Topology)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A star topology is defined as a central node <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> connected to a set of leaf nodes <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1},v_{2},\ldots,v_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1},v_{2},\ldots,v_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, where:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="deg(v_{c})=k,\quad deg(v_{i})=1\ \text{for all}\ i=1,\ldots,k." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for all</mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">deg(v_{c})=k,\quad deg(v_{i})=1\ \text{for all}\ i=1,\ldots,k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stars are typical of centralized libraries that provide core functionality to multiple dependent components.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Cycle Topology)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A cycle represents a circular dependency, where a closed path <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=(v_{1},v_{2},\ldots,v_{k},v_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=(v_{1},v_{2},\ldots,v_{k},v_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> exists, and every node <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected to exactly two others. Formally, a cycle is defined as:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{(v_{i},v_{i+1})\ |\ i=1,\ldots,k-1\}\cup\{(v_{k},v_{1})\}\subseteq E." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(v_{i},v_{i+1})\ |\ i=1,\ldots,k-1\}\cup\{(v_{k},v_{1})\}\subseteq E.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Cycles often signify complex interdependencies, which can make dependency resolution challenging.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TABLE IV</span>: </span><span id="S3.T4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Statistics of sub-topological structures in LLMSC dependency trees.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S3.T4.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T4.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T4.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T4.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Typology</span></td>
<td id="S3.T4.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T4.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Count</span></td>
<td id="S3.T4.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T4.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T4.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T4.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Chain Typology</td>
<td id="S3.T4.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">92</td>
<td id="S3.T4.5.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">datasets@3.2.2</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T4.5.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T4.5.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Triangle Typology</td>
<td id="S3.T4.5.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">5,903</td>
<td id="S3.T4.5.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">langsmith@0.3.11</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T4.5.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T4.5.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Diamond Typology</td>
<td id="S3.T4.5.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">6,895</td>
<td id="S3.T4.5.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">mcp@1.3.0</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T4.5.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T4.5.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Star Typology</td>
<td id="S3.T4.5.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">425</td>
<td id="S3.T4.5.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">tokencost@0.1.18</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T4.5.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T4.5.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Cycle Typology</td>
<td id="S3.T4.5.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">88</td>
<td id="S3.T4.5.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">tansformers@4.49.0</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prevalence of Substructures.</span>
Dependency trees in the LLMSC are composed of these primary sub-topological structures, with their prevalence summarized in <a href="#S3.T4" title="TABLE IV ‣ III-B Results ‣ III RQ1: Structure ‣ Understanding Large Language Model Supply Chain: Structure, Domain, and Vulnerabilities" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table IV</span></a>. Among these, diamonds are the most prevalent, with 6,895 instances, primarily representing shared libraries where intermediate dependencies are extensively reused. Triangles are the second most common structure, accounting for 5,903 instances, reflecting tightly coupled dependency clusters.
Star structures, with 425 instances, indicate highly centralized topologies where core packages directly support multiple dependent components. Chains, totaling 92 instances, are typically observed in smaller and simpler dependency trees with sequential dependencies. Lastly, 88 cycle structures were identified, representing rare but complex interdependencies that complicate dependency resolution and increase the risk of cascading failures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1.3:</span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">How are the various dependency trees interconnected within the LLMSC?</span>
The interconnectedness of dependency trees within the LLMSC arises from shared dependency nodes, common dependency paths, and varying degrees of tree coupling. These interconnections are essential for understanding the supply chain’s structural characteristics, as they highlight both the modularity and the potential propagation pathways for vulnerabilities across projects. Here, we analyze these factors in detail and summarize the key findings.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TABLE V</span>: </span><span id="S3.T5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Summary of interconnection metrics.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S3.T5.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T5.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T5.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T5.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metric</span></td>
<td id="S3.T5.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T5.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Value</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T5.5.2" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">
<td id="S3.T5.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T5.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">Total number of shared nodes</span></td>
<td id="S3.T5.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T5.5.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">2,767</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T5.5.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T5.5.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Nodes shared by exactly 2 trees</td>
<td id="S3.T5.5.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">660</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T5.5.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T5.5.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Nodes shared by 3–10 trees</td>
<td id="S3.T5.5.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">1,995</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T5.5.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T5.5.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Nodes shared by more than 10 trees</td>
<td id="S3.T5.5.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">112</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T5.5.6" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">
<td id="S3.T5.5.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T5.5.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">Total number of shared dependency paths</span></td>
<td id="S3.T5.5.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T5.5.6.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">311</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T5.5.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T5.5.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Paths shared by 2 trees</td>
<td id="S3.T5.5.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">46.30%</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T5.5.8" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T5.5.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Paths shared by 3–5 trees</td>
<td id="S3.T5.5.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">36.33%</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T5.5.9" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T5.5.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Tree pairs with at least one shared node</td>
<td id="S3.T5.5.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">1,768</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T5.5.10" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T5.5.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Tree pairs sharing fewer than 10 nodes</td>
<td id="S3.T5.5.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">1,487</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T5.5.11" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T5.5.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Tree pairs sharing more than 50 nodes</td>
<td id="S3.T5.5.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">75</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Shared Dependency Nodes.</span>
Shared nodes are components that appear in multiple dependency trees, serving as bridges that connect different projects. From our analysis, a total of 2,767 nodes are shared across at least two dependency trees, with the majority shared by only less than five trees (accounting for 73.2% of all shared nodes). As shown in <a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III-B Results ‣ III RQ1: Structure ‣ Understanding Large Language Model Supply Chain: Structure, Domain, and Vulnerabilities" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 3</span></a>, the distribution of shared nodes decreases sharply as the sharing scope increases: 854 nodes are shared by exactly three trees, 371 by four trees, and only 113 nodes are shared by more than 10 trees. These highly shared nodes often represent critical libraries or frameworks, such as the langflow-nightly@1.2.0 (shared across 24 trees) and raglight@1.3.5 (shared across 17 trees). These components form the backbone of the ecosystem by providing essential, reusable functionality. However, their centrality also makes them potential single points of failure.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2504.20763v1/shared.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/310;" width="405" height="310"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Distribution of shared dependency nodes and paths.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Shared Dependency Paths.</span>
Shared dependency paths, defined as sequences of dependencies that are reused across multiple trees, reflect common functional stacks or workflows within the LLMSC. Our analysis identified 311 shared paths of length greater than two (i.e., containing at least three nodes). Most of these paths (46.30%) are shared by exactly two trees, while a smaller percentage are shared among three to five trees (36.33%) . Only a handful of paths (58) are shared by more than five trees. These shared paths often correspond to specific frameworks or workflows that are widely adopted across projects. For example, the sequence “Interpret → Error‑Analysis → Model‑Assessment” is a recurring subpath in Responsible AI toolchains, while the chain “GraphQL Transformer → GraphQL API Construct → Data Construct” is frequently reused in projects on the AWS Amplify platform. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III-B Results ‣ III RQ1: Structure ‣ Understanding Large Language Model Supply Chain: Structure, Domain, and Vulnerabilities" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 3</span></a> illustrates the distribution of shared paths across trees, highlighting the concentration of path reuse in a small subset of workflows. Such shared paths represent reusable templates but also create interdependencies that could propagate issues if any part of the path fails or becomes incompatible.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS2.p12.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="122.77" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 122.77" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,122.77) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 116.86 C 0 120.12 2.64 122.77 5.91 122.77 L 471.47 122.77 C 474.73 122.77 477.38 120.12 477.38 116.86 L 477.38 5.91 C 477.38 2.64 474.73 0 471.47 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 116.86 C 1.97 119.03 3.73 120.8 5.91 120.8 L 471.47 120.8 C 473.65 120.8 475.41 119.03 475.41 116.86 L 475.41 5.91 C 475.41 3.73 473.65 1.97 471.47 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 16.47)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.37em;--ltx-fo-height:6.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="95.21" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 92.52)" width="434.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS2.p12.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.37em;">
<span id="S3.SS2.p12.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p12.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Answer to RQ1.<span id="S3.SS2.p12.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The LLMSC consists of 404 dependency trees, 79.7% of which have fewer than 5 nodes, while a few large trees and high-degree hubs dominate. Internally, diamond (54.2%) and triangle (46.4%) topologies are the main substructures. Moreover, 2,767 shared nodes and 311 shared paths indicate interconnections and potential risk across trees.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RQ2: Domain Distribution</span></h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Methodology</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate the domain composition of the LLMSC and its evolution, we focus on three key aspects: the static distribution of functional domains, the temporal evolution of these domains, and the hierarchical transitions of domains within dependency trees. First, we assign each package to a functional domain, such as “Data Pipelines,” “Training Frameworks,” and “Inference Tools,” based on its metadata and README content using a combination of keyword matching and the pre-trained classification model DeepSeek-V3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. Then, we analyze hierarchical domain transitions within dependency trees, treating each as a rooted directed acyclic graph (DAG) and tracing the flow of domains from root to leaf nodes. These analyses provide a comprehensive understanding of the LLMSC’s domain-level composition, its evolution over time, and the structural relationships between functional domains.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Results</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2.1:</span> <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">What is the domain distribution of the LLMSC, and how has it evolved over time?</span>
To understand the domain composition of the LLMSC and its temporal evolution, we analyzed the functional domains of LLM-related packages and tracked the growth trends across these domains over the past decade. The key findings are as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Domain Classification.</span> To analyze the domain distribution of the LLMSC, we first classified the retrieved packages using a heuristic LLM lifecycle framework inspired by prior research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>. This framework defines multiple stages in the LLM lifecycle and technical stack, including data preprocessing, model training, deployment, and post-deployment monitoring, each of which is supported by specialized tools and frameworks. Based on this lifecycle, we categorized all packages into 14 functional domains, such as “Data Pipeline,” “Training and Fine-tuning Frameworks,” “LLM Inference,” and “Orchestration Frameworks.” To assign packages to these domains, we employed DeepSeek-V3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, a pre-trained classification model that processes the README content and metadata of each package. The model automatically classified packages into the predefined categories based on their described functionalities, ensuring that each package was assigned to the most relevant domain.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Domain Distribution.</span> The static distribution, as shown in <a href="#S4.T6" title="TABLE VI ‣ IV-B Results ‣ IV RQ2: Domain Distribution ‣ Understanding Large Language Model Supply Chain: Structure, Domain, and Vulnerabilities" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table VI</span></a>, indicates that the LLMSC is primarily driven by a few key domains. “Plugins/External Tools” account for the largest proportion, comprising <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="44.26\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>44.26</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">44.26\%</annotation></semantics></math> (6,960 packages) of the ecosystem, reflecting the widespread use of auxiliary tools that extend or integrate with LLMs. “LLM Inference” is the second-largest domain at <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10.79\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10.79</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10.79\%</annotation></semantics></math> (1,696 packages), highlighting the importance of tools that enable efficient inference for pre-trained models. Other significant domains include “Data Pipeline” (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11.38\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>11.38</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11.38\%</annotation></semantics></math>, 1,789 packages), “Training and Fine-tuning Frameworks” (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.64\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.64</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.64\%</annotation></semantics></math>, 1,044 packages), and “LLM Gateway” (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.21\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7.21</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.21\%</annotation></semantics></math>, 1,134 packages), which collectively represent core functionalities for building, adapting, and deploying LLM-based applications. Smaller but specialized domains, such as “Logging &amp; LLMOps” (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.31\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.31</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.31\%</annotation></semantics></math>, 520 packages), “Vector Database” (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.30\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.30</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.30\%</annotation></semantics></math>, 205 packages), and “RAG Frameworks” (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.15\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.15</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.15\%</annotation></semantics></math>, 338 packages), address specific technical needs like model monitoring, data retrieval, and augmented generation workflows. Emerging categories such as “LLM Agent” and “LLM DSL/Programming” remain nascent, with only one package each, reflecting their early-stage development.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T6" class="ltx_table">
